        "content": "<p>I am struggling at the boss level of the coproduct world.</p>\n<p>In particular, my first five lines are:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"n\">constructor</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">intro</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h1</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">constructor</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">intro</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span>\n\n<span class=\"k\">have</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\"></annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></span></span></p>\n<p>I am able to “complete” the proof, but because of the red squiggle under the ‘a’ in ‘p1’ (and each time I use something similar to it) it isn’t letting it go through.</p>\n<p>My basic understanding is if I have <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⊔</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A \\sqcup B</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">A</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">⊔</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0502em;\">B</span></span></span></span>, then it acts like a logical or in some ways. Such that if I put in <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a : A</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">:</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">A</span></span></span></span>, then I can satisfy the statement.</p>\n<p>I think the best bet is to try and use Prod.lift and Sum.lift in the hypothesis, but <code>have/let p2 := Sum/Prod.lift h1</code> doesn’t seem to work out. Placing the line after <code>intro h1</code> I get the following error:</p>\n<p>Unknown constant <code>Prod.lift</code></p>\n<p>Any feedback that can help increase my understanding of the problem, or help change how I am viewing it is welcome.</p>",
