    {
        "content": "<p><code>h1 a</code> does not typecheck (is not \"well defined) because <code>h1</code> wants a term of type <code>A ⊕ B</code>, and <code>a</code> is of type <code>A</code>. You need to use the canonical function <code>A → A ⊕ B</code>.</p>",
        "id": 594917807,
        "sender_full_name": "Riccardo Brasca",
        "timestamp": 1778694261
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>You can find it in the Definition tab, under Sum.</p>",
        "id": 594917967,
        "sender_full_name": "Riccardo Brasca",
        "timestamp": 1778694301
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>my first three lines of a solution are</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"n\">constructor</span>\n<span class=\"n\">intro</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h</span>\n<span class=\"k\">let</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">hac</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">((</span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∘</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Sum</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">inl</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"m\">1</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 594918238,
        "sender_full_name": "Nikolai Iorgov",
        "timestamp": 1778694374
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"1078878\">@Lucas Orman</span> <code>A ⊕ B</code> is the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Disjoint_union\">disjoint union</a>. The only difference with the usual mathematical notion is that <code>A</code> is not a \"subset\" of <code>A ⊕ B</code>. If you have a term of type <code>A</code>, its type is cannot be <code>A ⊕ B</code>. On the other hand every time you have a term of type <code>A</code> you can get <code>Sum.inl a : A ⊕ B</code>, that mathematically is still <code>a</code>, but seen as an element of <code>A ⊕ B</code>.</p>",
        "id": 594919528,
        "sender_full_name": "Riccardo Brasca",
        "timestamp": 1778694721
    },
